WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.797
The second axis: reinforcement learning and test-time compute.

2
00:00:04.077 --> 00:00:13.848
By 2024 the cost of each step up in pretraining scale had become enormous, and two developments shifted attention to what happens after pretraining.

3
00:00:14.128 --> 00:00:16.496
Reinforcement learning on checkable tasks.

4
00:00:16.776 --> 00:00:28.573
Post-training had long included supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning from human feedback (the InstructGPT line of work by Ouyang and colleagues, 2022).

5
00:00:28.853 --> 00:00:36.725
The new step was reinforcement learning against verifiable rewards: math answers that can be checked, code that must pass tests.

6
00:00:37.005 --> 00:00:50.403
On 12 September 2024, OpenAI announced o1 and reported that its performance improved smoothly with more reinforcement-learning compute during training and with more time spent thinking at inference.

7
00:00:50.683 --> 00:01:11.376
In January 2025, DeepSeek published the R1 paper, which described reasoning behavior (long chains of thought, self-checking, backtracking) emerging from large-scale RL with rule-based rewards, including a variant trained by RL without supervised fine-tuning first, and released the weights openly.

8
00:01:11.656 --> 00:01:12.915
Test-time compute.

9
00:01:13.195 --> 00:01:18.037
If a model can use more tokens to think, you can trade inference cost for accuracy.

10
00:01:18.317 --> 00:01:38.136
Snell and colleagues (August 2024) studied how to spend that budget well and reported that a compute-optimal strategy could be more than four times as efficient as simple best-of-N sampling, and that on some problems a smaller model with extra test-time compute beat a model about 14 times larger.

11
00:01:38.416 --> 00:01:46.629
You already use this axis: the effort settings on current Claude, GPT, and Gemini models are dials on test-time compute.

12
00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:48.680
Two consequences matter for you.

13
00:01:48.960 --> 00:02:00.523
First, “how big is the model” no longer predicts capability or cost on its own; thinking tokens are billed as output, so the same model can be cheap or expensive depending on effort.

14
00:02:00.803 --> 00:02:04.941
Second, gains from RL are concentrated where rewards are checkable.

15
00:02:05.221 --> 00:02:16.869
That is one reason current models improved fastest at math and code, and part of why the debate below is so heated: does the improvement generalize to messy work where nothing checks the answer?
